Inverse cosine requires $-1\le x\le1$. On this interval, cosine undoes it, giving $y=x$.
The graph joins $(-1,-1)$ to $(1,1)$ with both endpoints included.
Domain: $[-1,1]$. Range: $[-1,1]$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=18mm,y=18mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{graphblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-1.5,0)--(1.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-1.5)--(0,1.6) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {-1,1} {
\draw[black] (\v,0.04)--(\v,-0.04) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};
\draw[black] (0.04,\v)--(-0.04,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};
}
\draw[graphblue,thick] (-1,-1)--(1,1);
\fill[graphblue] (-1,-1) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[graphblue] (1,1) circle (1.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

On $[0,\pi]$, the input is already in the range of inverse cosine, so $y=x$. On $[\pi,2\pi]$, the equivalent angle is $2\pi-x$. Cosine is even and repeats every $2\pi$, giving the remaining segments.
$y=\begin{cases}-x,&-\pi\le x\le0,\\x,&0\le x\le\pi,\\2\pi-x,&\pi\le x\le2\pi,\\x-2\pi,&2\pi\le x\le3\pi.\end{cases}$
Join $(-\pi,\pi)$, $(0,0)$, $(\pi,\pi)$, $(2\pi,0)$ and $(3\pi,\pi)$ with straight segments. All endpoints are included; the range is $[0,\pi]$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=15mm,y=24mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{graphblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-1.3,0)--(3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-0.25)--(0,1.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v/\lab in {-1/{-\pi},1/{\pi},2/{2\pi},3/{3\pi}} {
\draw[black] (\v,0.035)--(\v,-0.035) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\lab$};
}
\draw[black] (0.035,1)--(-0.035,1) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\pi$};
\node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[graphblue,thick] (-1,1)--(0,0)--(1,1)--(2,0)--(3,1);
\fill[graphblue] (-1,1) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[graphblue] (3,1) circle (1.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
